<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- THE REFERENCE SHAPE IS THIS PROJECT'S REASON TO EXIST. It models a
       runtime consumer of Sitrep.Contract that does NOT have the codegen
       package: exactly what a deployed assembly is, and exactly what nothing
       else here was.

       So: this project must NEVER reference Reinforced.Typings. Not to fix a
       red, not "just for this one test". If a test here needs it, the test is
       wrong or the contract is, and one of those is the finding.

       Note that Sitrep.Core.Tests requires the OPPOSITE and says so in its own
       csproj: it deliberately ships Reinforced.Typings.dll beside its test
       assembly because UnitDescriptorTests calls Assembly.GetTypes() over the
       netstandard2.0 contract and needs every type to resolve. Two projects,
       opposite requirements about the same DLL, one deliberate and documented.

       The accident is what cost us a month. ControlChannelDescriptor's property
       scan resolved Reinforced.Typings at runtime and threw where the package
       was correctly absent, which aborted every delayed command dispatch. It
       went unseen because the only suite exercising it had a stale copy of that
       DLL in bin, so thirteen tests passed while asserting nothing. -->
  <!-- Sitrep.Contract.TestSupport holds the reflection sweeps these [Fact]s
       assert on (UnitCoverageAssertion, ReckonabilityAssertion) and references
       nothing but Sitrep.Contract and xunit.assert, so it does not weaken the
       reference shape above: no Reinforced.Typings arrives with it. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Sitrep.Contract/Sitrep.Contract.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Sitrep.Contract.TestSupport/Sitrep.Contract.TestSupport.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
